     1	//! Village POI classification and density queries.
     2	//!
     3	//! Vanilla has no discrete "village" entity. It's read off POI density via
     4	//! `PoiManager`/`PoiTypes`
     5	//! (`net/minecraft/world/entity/ai/village/poi/{PoiManager,PoiTypes}.java`):
     6	//! every bed and every job-site block is registered as a POI at its block
     7	//! position, keyed by chunk section (`SectionPos.asLong`).
     8	//!
     9	//! - `ServerLevel.isCloseToVillage(pos, sectionDistance)`
    10	//!   (`ServerLevel.java` ~1551) is true when
    11	//!   `sectionsToVillage(pos) <= sectionDistance`, where `sectionsToVillage`
    12	//!   is the distance, in chunk sections, to the nearest section containing
    13	//!   an *occupied* POI tagged `#minecraft:village` (`home`, `meeting`, or
    14	//!   `#acquirable_job_site` -
    15	//!   `data/minecraft/tags/point_of_interest_type/village.json`), capped at
    16	//!   `PoiManager.MAX_VILLAGE_DISTANCE = 6`. The underlying
    17	//!   `SectionTracker`/`DynamicGraphMinFixedPoint` BFS
    18	//!   (`SectionTracker.java` `checkNeighborsAfterUpdate`/`getComputedLevel`)
    19	//!   propagates over the full 3x3x3 section neighborhood at uniform cost 1
    20	//!   per step, so the resulting distance metric is Chebyshev distance (in
    21	//!   sections) to the nearest source section, not Manhattan or Euclidean.
    22	//! - `CatSpawner.spawnInVillage` (`CatSpawner.java` line 47) additionally
    23	//!   requires `getCountInRange(HOME, pos, 48, Occupancy.IS_OCCUPIED) > 4`:
    24	//!   more than 4 *claimed* beds within a 48-block sphere
    25	//!   (`PoiManager.getInRange` prefilters with an axis-aligned square via
    26	//!   `getInSquare`, then applies `distSqr(center) <= radius*radius`, which
    27	//!   includes the Y axis).
    28	//!
    29	//! Villagers now claim POIs with a ticket system (`PoiRecord.freeTickets`/
    30	//! `maxTickets`, `pumpkin_world::poi::{PoiEntry, PoiStorage}`) instead of
    31	//! just existing at density-queryable positions - see `World::acquire_poi`/
    32	//! `release_poi` (vanilla `PoiManager.take`/`release`, driven by
    33	//! `AcquirePoi`/`Villager.releasePoi`). This module's own density queries
    34	//! (`sectionsToVillage`, `getCountInRange`) therefore use the real
    35	//! `Occupancy` filter below rather than a stand-in.
    36	//!
    37	//! `Occupancy::HasSpace` vs `Occupancy::IsOccupied` are **two different
    38	//! filters for two different purposes** and must not be conflated
    39	//! (`PoiManager.Occupancy` in vanilla): claiming a POI (e.g. a villager
    40	//! grabbing a bed) needs `HasSpace` (`freeTickets > 0` - there's a ticket
    41	//! left to give out); village-density checks (is this area an actual,
    42	//! lived-in village) need `IsOccupied` (`freeTickets != maxTickets` - the POI
    43	//! is *actually in use*, not merely present). A meeting point
    44	//! (`maxTickets = 32`) can be both simultaneously.
    45	//!
    46	//! Bed (`HOME`), job-site, and meeting-point (bell) acquisition are all wired up
    47	//! (`VillagerEntity::mob_tick` calls `World::acquire_poi`/`acquire_poi_where`/`release_poi`
    48	//! for all three), so `sections_to_village`'s job-site/meeting contribution is meaningful
    49	//! whenever a villager has actually claimed one - not merely whenever the block exists. A
    50	//! village with claimed beds/job sites/bells registers correctly; a freshly-generated,
    51	//! never-visited village (no villager has claimed anything yet) will not, which matches
    52	//! vanilla's own `isVillageCenter` requiring an *occupied* POI, not merely an existing one.
    53	//!
    54	//! Existing worlds with POI entries persisted before this ticket system
    55	//! landed have `free_tickets == 0` on disk (the prior code always wrote
    56	//! `0`, regardless of type). Since there is no version marker to tell a
    57	//! legitimately-claimed `0` apart from that legacy default, those entries
    58	//! load as permanently unclaimable (`has_space() == false`) and permanently
    59	//! "occupied" (`is_occupied() == true`) until the block is broken and
    60	//! replaced (which re-adds the POI via `World::set_block_state`, resetting
    61	//! `free_tickets` to `max_tickets`). This is a one-time migration quirk on
    62	//! pre-existing saves, not a bug in new POI registrations.
    63	//!
    64	//! Registry population: entries are added/removed incrementally at
    65	//! `World::set_block_state`, the single block-mutation chokepoint, mirroring
    66	//! vanilla's `PoiManager` reacting to `onBlockStateChange`. Structures placed
    67	//! directly by world generation (freshly generated villages) write blocks
    68	//! into chunk sections in `pumpkin-world` without going through that
    69	//! chokepoint, so they are not backfilled into the registry. Vanilla's
    70	//! equivalent backfill runs off chunk load
    71	//! (`PoiManager.checkConsistencyWithBlocks`/`updateFromSection`), which would
    72	//! require hooking chunk loading in `pumpkin-world` - out of scope for this
    73	//! pass; a freshly-generated, never-modified village will not register as
    74	//! "close to village" until a bed or job-site block is placed or broken.
    75	//!
    76	//! Storage reuses `World::portal_poi`
    77	//! (`pumpkin_world::poi::PoiStorage`), which already persists region-keyed
    78	//! POI entries to the same `poi/` folder vanilla's `PoiManager` writes to.
    79	//! Vanilla keeps every POI type (portals, beds, job sites, meeting,
    80	//! beehives, ...) in one registry, so sharing the store here matches
    81	//! vanilla's structure rather than compromising it.
    82	//!
    83	//! Bed head/foot: vanilla only registers the `BedPart.HEAD` half of a bed as
    84	//! a `HOME` POI (`PoiTypes.java` ~113, `register(..., HOME, BEDS, 1, 1)`
    85	//! filtered to `BedPart.HEAD` states). `classify_block` now takes the
    86	//! block's state id and applies the same filter, so each bed resolves to
    87	//! exactly one POI record - required for ticket claiming to make sense (a
    88	//! villager must never be able to claim the foot half as if it were its own
    89	//! separate bed).
